WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.283
An observation mission should begin with a specific question.

00:00:04.283 --> 00:00:13.870
Examples include documenting construction progress, assessing storm damage, confirming whether a route is obstructed, or recording the condition of an authorized asset.

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:18.561
The question determines the scene, detail, timing, and collection method.

00:00:18.561 --> 00:00:21.621
Define the area of interest precisely.

00:00:21.621 --> 00:00:27.128
A smaller bounded area reduces flight time, data volume, and privacy impact.

00:00:27.128 --> 00:00:35.083
Identify boundaries that should not be crossed or recorded, including nearby homes, sensitive facilities, people, or unrelated operations.

00:00:35.083 --> 00:00:36.919
Specify required detail.

00:00:36.919 --> 00:00:44.466
A broad overview may show access routes and terrain, while an inspection may require closer authorized views.

00:00:44.466 --> 00:00:52.420
More detail is not automatically better; it increases storage, processing, and the chance of capturing unnecessary sensitive information.

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:55.072
Choose the relevant time window.

00:00:55.072 --> 00:01:01.803
Lighting, traffic, weather, vegetation, tides, work schedules, and temporary obstructions can change what is visible.

00:01:01.803 --> 00:01:08.942
The observation should occur when it can answer the question safely and without creating avoidable impact.

00:01:08.942 --> 00:01:12.002
Record environmental constraints and decision criteria.

00:01:12.002 --> 00:01:20.364
State what would make the data usable, what conditions would require rescheduling, and which uncertainties the imagery cannot resolve.

00:01:20.364 --> 00:01:26.279
Avoid redesigning the mission in flight without a renewed risk and privacy review.

00:01:26.279 --> 00:01:29.747
Also define what should not be collected.

00:01:29.747 --> 00:01:34.438
Purpose limitation protects people and improves analysis by reducing noise.

00:01:34.438 --> 00:01:41.577
A clear objective creates a defensible link between the mission, each image, and the final report.

00:01:41.577 --> 00:01:45.045
A useful objective also names the decision.

00:01:45.045 --> 00:01:55.855
If the audience must choose whether to send a ground inspection team, the imagery should support that choice instead of producing a general scenic survey.
